title "A Tour of tekx"
subtitle "Mathematics that means something"
author "Ada Lovelace"
date today

// A text macro, a block macro and a new kind of theorem.
define golden as "the golden ratio"
define block aside(title = "Aside")
  quote
    **{title}.** {body}
define principle like theorem titled "Principle"

abstract
  This document is written in tekx. It shows how prose, structure and
  mathematics read naturally in source form, and how one source yields
  typeset pages, MathML, spoken mathematics and clean copy text.

section Prose and mathematics #intro

Let us begin with {golden}, $phi = (1 + sqrt 5)/2$. It satisfies

    phi^2 = phi + 1 #golden

which a screen reader speaks as "phi squared equals phi plus 1", and which
copies out of the PDF as `φ² = φ + 1`. Paste that back into a tekx file and you
get the same formula. Long words such as incomprehensibilities are hyphenated
and still copy as one word; see @fib and <https://example.org>.

theorem "Pythagoras" #pyth
  For a right triangle with legs $a$, $b$ and hypotenuse $c$,

      a^2 + b^2 = c^2

proof
  Arrange four copies of the triangle inside a square of side $a + b$.

principle "Least surprise"
  A formula should read as it is spoken.

aside "Note"
  Equation @golden determines $phi$ up to sign, and @pyth is older than
  Pythagoras.

section Structure

- Lists, with $x in RR$ inside.
- Items long enough to wrap across more than one line keep their hanging
  indentation.
  1. Nested and numbered.
  2. Like this.

| Symbol | Name | Meaning |
|:------:|------|---------|
| $NN$   | `NN` | natural numbers |
| $RR$   | `RR` | real numbers |

```
tekx tour.tekx      # writes tour.pdf, silently
```

    f(x) = cases
      x^2   if x >= 0
      -x    otherwise

    (a + b)^2 = a^2 + 2 a b + b^2
              = b^2 + 2 a b + a^2

references
  fib: N. N. Vorobiev, *Fibonacci Numbers*, Birkhäuser, 2002.
